Background and definitions. A fuzzy metric space (in the sense of George and Veeramani) is a triple (X, M, *) where X is a nonempty set, * is a continuous t-norm, and M: X × X × (0, ∞) → [0,1] satisfies, for all x, y, z ∈ X and t, s > 0:
(i) M(x, y, t) > 0;
(ii) M(x, y, t) = 1 if and only if x = y;
(iii) M(x, y, t) = M(y, x, t);
(iv) M(x, y, t) * M(y, z, s) ≤ M(x, z, t + s);
(v) the function M(x, y, ·): (0, ∞) → [0,1] is continuous.
The topology induced by M has as a basis the open balls B(x, r, t) = {y ∈ X : M(x, y, t) > 1 − r} for r ∈ (0,1), t > 0. A sequence (x_n) is Cauchy if for every r ∈ (0,1) and t > 0 there is N with M(x_n, x_m, t) > 1 − r for all n, m ≥ N; the space is complete if every Cauchy sequence converges. A fuzzy metric space is called completable if it is isometrically dense in some complete fuzzy metric space; it is known that not every fuzzy metric space is completable.

Two fuzzy metric spaces (X, M, *) and (Y, N, *) are isometric if there is a bijection f: X → Y with N(f(x), f(y), t) = M(x, y, t) for all x, y ∈ X and t > 0. On the set CFM of isometry classes of compact fuzzy metric spaces (with a fixed continuous t-norm *), the fuzzy Gromov–Hausdorff metric M_GH is defined by
   M_GH([X], [Y], t) = sup { H_{Z,L}(i(X), j(Y), t) },
where the supremum is over all fuzzy metric spaces (Z, L, *) and isometric embeddings i: X ↪ Z, j: Y ↪ Z, and H denotes the Hausdorff fuzzy metric on nonempty compact subsets of Z. This makes (CFM, M_GH, *) into a fuzzy metric space.

Problem. Is the fuzzy metric space (CFM, M_GH, *) completable? If so, can the elements of its completion be naturally identified with (isometry classes of) complete separable fuzzy metric spaces?

